# Testing (TDD — Tests Before Implementation)

Tests are written from spec acceptance criteria BEFORE implementation code exists. They define the verification contract.

## TDD workflow
1. Read the spec — every filled section, not just Functional. Multi-perspective spec model means test scope comes from MORE than just ACs:
   - **Functional** — every AC, every edge case (always)
   - **Testing** — explicit test requirements (axe scans, perf tests, manual passes)
   - **Security** — testable mitigations (rate limit, validation, auth checks)
   - **Accessibility** — testable a11y requirements (axe scan, ARIA roles, keyboard navigation)
   - **Performance** — testable SLAs (response time, bundle size — usually a separate test type)
   - **Privacy** — testable data-handling behaviors
   - **Analytics** — testable event firing
   - **Localization** — testable per-locale rendering
   Skip sections marked Not applicable / Standard applies.
2. Write tests that describe expected behavior (code doesn't exist yet)
3. Run tests — they should all fail (red phase)
4. Commit tests with `test:` prefix
5. Next phase is docs (write user-facing docs, if any pre-implementable docs are in the spec) — then implement code to make tests pass

## Structure
- One test file per feature area
- Descriptive names that read as behavior descriptions
- Arrange → Act → Assert structure
- Group with describe/context blocks

## What to test
- User flows matching spec acceptance criteria
- Edge cases listed in specs
- Testable requirements from Security, Accessibility, Performance, Privacy, Analytics, Localization sections
- Error states and empty states
- View transitions, form submissions, navigation

## What NOT to test
- Implementation details or internal state
- CSS classes or styling specifics
- Third-party library internals
- Anything not in the spec

## Mocking
- Mock ALL external services — tests must not depend on services being up
- Mock responses must match actual API data structures
- Use the project's mocking approach (check AGENTS.md for conventions)

## Test data
- Use fake but realistic data
- Never use real credentials or PII
